# --- Python ---
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/

# --- environments ---
.venv/
venv/

# --- build output ---
# NOT `build/` wholesale: build/build.py, build/native.py and
# build/installer.iss are source and must be committed. Only the
# generated output is ignored.
build/dist/
build/work/
build/installer/
build/release/
dist/
*.spec

# build/native.py unpacks and compiles the Tier 2 libraries here. It is
# fetched-and-verified third-party source plus CMake output -- 132 MB of
# it, and every generated Makefile records the ABSOLUTE build path, so
# committing it would publish the build machine's directory layout. This
# tree is reproducible from native.py whenever it is needed.
build/thirdparty/

# --- Tier 2 shared libraries: BUILT, never committed ------------------
# native.py compiles these from upstream source pinned by SHA-256 and
# installs them here. They are deliberately not in version control, for
# the reason native.py states in full: a prebuilt binary inside a
# security tool is one nobody can account for, and "a suspicious user
# can run this script and compare" only holds while the comparison is
# possible. A committed DLL removes it -- the reader would have to trust
# that our binary matches the pinned sources, which makes us the random
# host that argument was written against.
#
# There is a practical half too. These are Windows DLLs, useless on
# Linux, and having them in the tree is exactly what caused a Linux
# build to bundle them and lose every Tier 2 format.
#
# Cost: cloning and building on Windows yields Tier 1 only until
# native.py is run. The application says so plainly rather than failing
# oddly -- `pakt formats` reports "tier 2: unavailable" and names what
# is missing. Anyone building from source can run one script; everyone
# else installs a release, which ships them.
app/assets/native/

# --- generated benchmark input ---
# benchmarks/corpus.py builds these from pinned or seeded inputs. They
# are INPUT to the harness, not results: sourcetree is a copy of this
# very source tree and sitepackages a copy of the venv, so committing
# them would duplicate the repository inside itself. RESULTS.md and
# results.json are the outputs worth keeping and are NOT ignored.
benchmarks/corpora/

# --- compiled engine ---
# The .pyd and the C it is generated from are proprietary output. The
# C in particular is a near-complete transcription of the engine source
# and must never reach the public repository.
*.pyd
*.so
*.c

# --- proprietary: must never enter the public repository ---
# The routing engine and the trained dictionary corpus live in the
# private compakt-engine repository. These entries are a backstop in
# case anyone copies one in by accident.
core/compressor.py
dictionaries/
*.pakt-dict

# --- local ---
.vscode/
.idea/
*.log
